    \u0432\u0435\u0440\u0441\u0438\u0438 \u043c\u0438\u0440\u043d\u043e\u0439 \u043a\u043e\u043d\u0447\u0438\u043d\u044b)
    \u043e\u043d \u043e\u0441\u0442\u0430\u0432\u0438\u043b \u0437\u0430\u0432\u0435\u0449\u0430\u043d\u0438\u0435
    \u0443\u0447\u0435\u043d\u0438\u043a\u0430\u043c, \u0433\u0434\u0435 \u043f\u0440\u043e\u0441\u0438\u043b
    \u0438\u0445 \u043a\u043e\u0440\u043c\u0438\u0442\u044c \u0435\u0433\u043e \u043a\u043e\u0442\u0430
    \u0438 \u043a\u0443\u043f\u0438\u0442\u044c \u043d\u043e\u0432\u044b\u0439 \u0445\u0438\u0442\u043e\u043d,
    \u0442\u0430\u043a \u043a\u0430\u043a \u0441\u0442\u0430\u0440\u044b\u0439 \u00ab\u0438\u0437\u043d\u043e\u0441\u0438\u043b\u0441\u044f\u00bb.
    \u042d\u0442\u043e \u0441\u0442\u0440\u0430\u043d\u043d\u0430\u044f \u043c\u0435\u043b\u043e\u0447\u044c
    \u043d\u0430 \u0444\u043e\u043d\u0435 \u0435\u0433\u043e \u0432\u0435\u043b\u0438\u0447\u0438\u044f
    \u043f\u043e\u043a\u0430\u0437\u044b\u0432\u0430\u0435\u0442, \u0447\u0442\u043e \u0434\u0430\u0436\u0435
    \u0432\u0435\u043b\u0438\u043a\u0438\u0439 \u0444\u0438\u043b\u043e\u0441\u043e\u0444 \u043e\u0441\u0442\u0430\u0432\u0430\u043b\u0441\u044f
    \u0447\u0435\u043b\u043e\u0432\u0435\u043a\u043e\u043c \u0441 \u0431\u044b\u0442\u043e\u0432\u044b\u043c\u0438
    \u0437\u0430\u0431\u043e\u0442\u0430\u043c\u0438."
  interestingFactsEn: "Pythagore's life has grown so big legends that it is sometimes impossible to separate historical truth from fiction, and it is in this grey zone that the most exciting and curly facts lie. Here is a collection of the most unusual and amusing details of the life of the great philosopher and his school.\n\nHe said: \"I am not a wise man, I am only seeking wisdom.\" He compared life to the Olympic Games: some come to compete, others to trade, and others (the best) to just see and understand how things work.\n\nThe Pythagore School had the strictest rule: the first five years of schooling were to keep the students silent; they could only listen to the teacher's lectures because of the curtain, without seeing his face, and never ask questions or argue; this was to clear the mind of old prejudices.Later, when the student became a \"mathematician\" (recognisant), his main oath before the dedication of the teaching was to say, \"Av·os e·fa\" — \"Sum said\" — and that authority was so unconscionable that the phrase became a wingman. If the students heard an argument that was contrary to Pifagor's words, they would simply close their eyes and say, \"The same said\", stopping the discussion.\n\nBeans are the most notorious and strange taboo of the Pythagoreans: taboos on beans (fasole) were so severe that the violation was punishable by death. (In historians' versions) several causes are common, all of which are common: Mystic: Pythagoras believed in the relocation of souls and believed that the souls of the dead could live in beans (because of a hollow tube inside the stem, through which the souls are said to enter the ground).Political: Bobs were used to vote in democratic assemblies (white and black stones). Pythagoras, who was an aristocrat, may have hated democracy and banned beans as a symbol of crowd control.Allergic: There is a hypothesis that Pythagore had a genetic disease (favis) in which the use of beans caused the destruction of erythrocytes and could have been fatal. Legend is that the beans were the cause of its death: when he fled the pursuers, he fled the field with the beans, but when he saw them, he stopped and said, \"It's better to die than to attack the beans,\" and then he was caught by the enemies.\n\nThe discovery of irrational numbers (and murder for him) by the Pythagoreys worshipped numbers and believed that everything in the world could be expressed by the attitude of whole numbers. They were convinced of the harmony and \"measureability\" of the world. However, one student, Hippas of Metapont, mathematically proved that the diagonal square was not commensurate with its side (i.e., G2 is an endless non-recurrent fraction).Instead of enjoying a scientific breakthrough, the Pythagoreys were terrified.\n\nThe Sources claim that Pythagoras was a very spectacular man, wearing white, long, low-colored garments (as opposed to the plain Greeks in the shorts), which made him look decorated, and that there was a legend that Pifagor had a \"gold thigh\".When he was at the Olympic Games in front of the crowd, he allegedly raised his clothes and showed the assembly that his leg was made of pure gold, which was to confirm his divine origin (connect to Apollo). Some historians believe it was just a drama trick using a gilded prosthetic or a metal plate sewn under his skin.\n\n♪ The genius memory and past lives of Pythagoras believed in the resettling of souls (metempsychosis) and claimed to remember all his previous lives ♪ He told his disciples that he was 1 ♪ ♪ A son of Hermes ♪ Ephalid ♪ who could remember everything ♪ ♪ Two ♪ Trojan hero Evforb (who was killed under Troy's walls ♪ ♪ A fish named Pir ♪ ♪ ♪ Beautiful woman named Aleta ♪In order to prove the latter, he allegedly entered the temple of Gera in Argos and recognized his old shield, which had been there since the Trojan War — it kept the name of Euphorba on it.\n\nThe gold bowl and hygiene was one of the symbols of the Pythagoreys, the Golden Cup, but it was not a cup, but a bronze vessel that was placed at intersections, and the fact is that only those who knew the exact geometry of the volume could find water in it. He also devised an original way to combat epidemics (as a result of their contagious nature).He ordered the city's people to go out on the streets with laurels and \"clean the air\" through choir singing and music, which actually helped to spray the phytocides and create psychological comfort.\n\nThe first vegetarian and the vegan know-how of Pythagore was a certain vegetarian.By the way, it was the Pythagoreys who began producing \"artificial meat\" from wheat and beans, roasting them to a crisp crust to replace the sacrificed animals, because they considered it barbarous to offer blood sacrifice to the gods.\n\n▪ Musical treatment (music therapy) by Pythagoras treated the soul and body diseases with music. He specifically wrote melodies against rage and anger, and against passion. In the morning, he ordered his students to sing bright, complicated melodies to stimulate mind, and in the evening he was calm, snatching to clear his mind of day-to-day disturbances. He said that he had one day personally atone a drunken bourgeon, simply changing the musical light (fluith) in the house where he was singing.\n\nWhen asked what was best for a man after his life, he said, \"Do not ask questions about what you have no questions about.\" There is a funny chicken who left a will to feed his cat and buy a new chiton before he died (in the version of the peaceful death).This strange little thing, against the background of his greatness, shows that even the great philosopher remained a man of domestic concern."
